#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sigmond-sdr-recover — reset the hub port of a vanished or wedged RX-888 and
bring the station back.

The RX-888 recurrently disappears from the USB bus, and sometimes stays on it
with its stream wedged.  A radiod restart or a USB rebind does not help.  This
helper switches the card's hub port off and on ("port reset"), waits for the
FX3 to re-enumerate, and restarts the station in the one order that works.

⛔ It is a PORT RESET, not a power cycle.  A hub that reports ppps is not
thereby a hub that cuts VBUS: AC0G-B4's two hubs (VIA 2109:0817/2817 and
Anker 291a:8307) read 4.98 V through every `uhubctl -a off`, the LEDs stayed
lit, and the card re-enumerated by itself with the port still "off" (10-14).
The off drops the data link and so reloads the FX3 firmware, and that cleared
a stream wedge on 10-11.  It never clears the cold-start latch (no SuperSpeed
link, kernel -71 or -110): a hand replug did, every time.  04b4:00f3 on return
proves nothing about power, so nothing here reads it that way.  A port counts
as switching only when the station says a cut was watched
(`[radiod] recover_port_verified = true`); otherwise it reads "switching
unverified".

A USB3 hub presents each physical port twice, a SuperSpeed half and a USB2
half, and the RX888 streams on the SuperSpeed half.  The learn step records
which halves uhubctl accepts (port_halves).  When it reaches only the USB2
half, no reset can clear the card, so a vanish, a latch or a wedge raises the
replug alarm at once, with no reset tried (v3.73 row 9; AC0G-B4 10-15).  A
station that measured its hub holding VBUS through a cut says so with
`[radiod] recover_port_data_only = true`: a reset there still clears a stream
wedge, and a vanish or a latch asks for the replug at once (port_power,
reset_can_help).

After a reset the return is classified.  Card back as 00f1 on a SuperSpeed
path with radiod ready reads "recovered by port reset".  Anything else (USB2
half only, -71/-110 in the kernel log, bootloader only, never back) raises ONE
alarm per episode, "needs a hand replug at the RX888", and the helper stops
resetting until the card comes back healthy.  It never resets a port that
holds anything but the RX-888.

Two rules govern the design, and AC0G-B4 taught both the hard way.

**Fire on the cause, never on a consequence.**  timestd-hpps-watchdog restarted
the recorder four times on the night of 2026-08-29 because a chrony refclock
had gone stale.  The actual cause was low carrier-to-noise density, which no
restart can mend, so it churned the station and fixed nothing.  This helper
therefore triggers on the card's own failure only: absent from the USB bus, or
present with radiod restart-looping on USB stall lines (a wedge, v3.72).  A
present card with a healthy radiod is never reset, however unhappy anything
downstream looks.  Its sibling sigmond-radiod-watchdog restarts a radiod that
hung after a stall; this helper resets the hub port when the stall repeats.

**Declare the consumers; never discover them by hand.**  A recorder left
running against a restarted radiod holds a stale RTP anchor and fails quietly.
wspr-recorder ran that way for two days, logging a timing fault every two
minutes that nobody acted on, and it was missed again during the recovery
because the operator expanded the consumer set with a glob typed at the time.
The set now lives in topology.toml, and a pattern that matches nothing is
reported rather than skipped.

Order matters and is not negotiable: radiod first, then everything that reads
its RTP streams.  Starting a consumer before radiod serves gives it an anchor
that is wrong for the rest of its life.

**Learn the port while the card is there; you cannot learn it once it is gone.**
Nothing in bring-up writes `[radiod] recover_hub / recover_port` to
topology.toml, so on every appliance this helper used to exit at once with
"no hub to cycle" (AI6VN 2026-09-05).  Now, on each run with the card present,
it locates the RX-888 in sysfs, walks up to the hub port it hangs off, asks
uhubctl whether that port can be switched, and persists the answer.  When the
card later vanishes, the persisted hub/port is what gets reset.  A hub that
cannot be switched is reported once and monitored, never reset: the operator
learns the card is unrecoverable from software *before* it fails, not after.
Explicit topology.toml values still win over discovery.

State persists in /var/lib/sigmond/sdr-recover.json, including the latch
("latched", "replug_alarm_at") that stops resets after a failed return.  The
latch clears only when the card is back as 00f1 at SuperSpeed with radiod
healthy, so a reboot does not forget that a hand replug is owed.

**Manual power control: --off / --on.**  Everything above is automatic and
brief.  Judging a hub needs the opposite: the radio down and STAYING down,
long enough to look at the RX-888's LED, swap a cable, or move the card.
That is what the WsprDaemon pair `wd -U off` / `wd -U on` does, and
`--off` / `--on` are its sigmond equivalent.

    sudo /usr/local/sbin/sigmond-sdr-recover --off      # radio down, held
    sudo /usr/local/sbin/sigmond-sdr-recover --status    # what is held, since when
    sudo /usr/local/sbin/sigmond-sdr-recover --on       # ports back, station back

`--off` refuses unless uhubctl can switch EVERY half of the card's jack and
nothing else shares it, stops the consumers and then radiod, switches both
halves off, and confirms the card actually left the bus — "I ran uhubctl off"
is not "the port is off".  It then writes a hold to
/var/lib/sigmond/sdr-hold.json, and while that hold stands both the recovery
timer here and sigmond-radiod-watchdog stand down, so neither turns the port
back on under the operator's hands.  `--on` switches the same ports on with
the SuperSpeed half first, waits for the card to be READY, restarts radiod
and its consumers, and clears the hold — and KEEPS the hold if the port or
the card does not get there, because a dark port needs a human and not a
watchdog.

⛔ READY MEANS 04b4:00f1 AT SUPERSPEED, NOT "SOMETHING IS ON THE BUS".
After a real VBUS cut the card's FIRST appearance is its FX3 bootloader,
04b4:00f3 at 480 Mb/s on the USB2 half; `rx888_boot` then loads the firmware
and it re-enumerates as 00f1 at 5000 on the SuperSpeed half seconds later.
Taking that first sighting as arrival started radiod against the bootloader
on AI6VN (2026-10-26 21:42:49Z) and radiod exited status=66/NOINPUT — it
recovered only because systemd's `Restart=` tried again 11 s later, which is
what kept the fault invisible.  So the bootloader is waited THROUGH, a card
that came back at 480 with its firmware gets one link blip, and a card that
never reaches 00f1 at 5000 keeps the hold and leaves radiod stopped, naming
which of the three ways it failed.

A station with more than one RX-888 must name the card: `--serial <SERIAL>`.
`--off` with two cards and no serial is refused, never guessed.  (The
automatic path above still watches one card per host; see
tasks/plan-multi-rx888-sources.md.)
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import fcntl
import fnmatch
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Iterable, List, Optional, Sequence, Tuple

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)),
                                "..", "lib"))
from sigmond.paths import LIFECYCLE_LOCK  # noqa: E402
from sigmond import sdr_events  # noqa: E402
from sigmond.radiod_ready import READY_SETTLE_S  # noqa: E402

# The card blips off the bus for a second or two whenever it re-enumerates —
# including during its own recovery.  Cycling inside that window would
# interrupt the very thing we are waiting for.
ABSENT_GRACE_S = 30

# If a reset did not bring it back, another one straight away will not either.
# A reset loop is worse than a dead SDR: it denies an operator the stable
# state they need in order to diagnose.
CYCLE_COOLDOWN_S = 900

# A present-but-wedged card (radiod restart-looping on USB transfer failures)
# gets a port reset at most once per WEDGE_RESET_COOLDOWN_S.  A reset costs
# about two minutes of station downtime and reloads the FX3; if one did not
# clear the wedge, a second within half an hour will not, while ND's real
# stalls came hours apart (three in 22 h), so 30 minutes loses none of them.
WEDGE_RESET_COOLDOWN_S = 1800
WEDGE_WINDOW_S = 600           # how far back the radiod journal is read
WEDGE_MIN_HITS = 3             # stall lines in that window
# An NRestarts delta counts only when its previous sample is this fresh (two
# ticks): a card that returned by itself must never be reset on a stale delta.
NRESTART_SAMPLE_MAX_AGE_S = 90
# radiod's own words for a stalled stream.  "USB xfer failures +0" is its
# routine counter and must not match, so the count must be non-zero.  Stall
# lines alone are not a wedge (a streaming radiod can show a non-zero
# counter): they need restart evidence beside them.
#
# B4's cold-boot latch (2026-10-14, 557 cycles in 1.5 h) shows none of the
# stream-stall words: radiod fails at init, exits 66, systemd restarts it.
STALL_RE = re.compile(r"No rx888 data for|USB xfer failures \+[1-9]|"
                      r"rx888_usb_init\(\) failed|device setup returned -\d+")
EXIT_RE = re.compile(r"status=6/ABRT|signal=ABRT|Aborted \(core dumped\)|"
                     r"status=66/NOINPUT|Scheduled restart job")
# What the kernel says of the cold-start latch.
LATCH_RE = re.compile(r"error -(71|110)\b")
SUPERSPEED_MBPS = 5000

RX888_IDS = ("04b4:00f1", "04b4:00f3")     # running, and the FX3 bootloader
STATE_PATH = Path("/var/lib/sigmond/sdr-recover.json")
# A manual --off writes here and --on clears it.  While a serial is
# held, every automatic path in this helper AND in
# sigmond-radiod-watchdog leaves that radio alone: the operator took
# the port down on purpose and a watchdog "recovering" it would turn
# the port back on under their hands.
HOLD_PATH = Path("/var/lib/sigmond/sdr-hold.json")
UHUBCTL = "/usr/sbin/uhubctl"              # NOT on a non-root PATH
CONFIG_PATH = Path("/etc/sigmond/topology.toml")
SYSFS_USB = Path("/sys/bus/usb/devices")
# Re-ask uhubctl this often even when nothing moved: a hub swap behind the same
# devpath, or a fixed udev permission, should not need a state-file reset.
REVERIFY_S = 24 * 3600


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Located:
    """Where the RX-888 sits right now: the sysfs devpath ("4-1.4"), the hub
    and port uhubctl needs ("4-1", "4"), and the card's serial and link speed."""
    devpath: str
    hub: str
    port: str
    serial: str
    speed: str
    # The same physical port seen from the other half of a USB3 hub (sysfs
    # `peer`): uhubctl refuses the SuperSpeed hub's own location for some
    # hubs but takes the USB2 companion's, and cycles both halves from there
    # (VIA VL817 in a Lenovo dock: `-l 4-1` fails, `-l 2-1` works).
    peer_hub: Optional[str] = None
    peer_port: Optional[str] = None
    product: str = ""       # idProduct: "00f1" running, "00f3" FX3 bootloader
    devnum: str = ""        # changes on every re-enumeration (a hand replug)


def split_devpath(devpath: str) -> Optional[Tuple[str, str]]:
    """sysfs devpath → (uhubctl hub location, port).

    "4-1.4"  → ("4-1", "4")   card on port 4 of the hub at bus 4 port 1
    "4-1.2.3"→ ("4-1.2", "3") nested hub
    "6-2"    → ("6", "2")     card straight on the root hub (uhubctl names a
                              root hub by its bus number)
    """
    if not devpath or ":" in devpath or devpath.startswith("usb"):
        return None
    if "." in devpath:
        hub, port = devpath.rsplit(".", 1)
    else:
        bus, _, port = devpath.partition("-")
        if not port:
            return None
        hub = bus
    if not port.isdigit():
        return None
    return hub, port


def parse_uhubctl(stdout: str, port: str) -> Optional[bool]:
    """Read `uhubctl -l HUB -p PORT` output.

    True  — the hub is listed and shows the port: uhubctl only lists hubs whose
            ports it can switch, so the listing IS the capability check.
    False — no compatible hub at that location: try the peer location before
            concluding the port cannot be switched from software.
    None  — uhubctl could not look (permissions / not installed): unknown.
    """
    low = stdout.lower()
    if "permission" in low or "no such file" in low or "command not found" in low:
        return None
    if "no compatible devices" in low:
        return False
    for line in stdout.splitlines():
        if line.strip().lower().startswith(f"port {port}:"):
            return True
    return False if "current status for hub" in low else None


def peer_of(devpath: str, root: Path = SYSFS_USB) -> Optional[Tuple[str, str]]:
    """The other logical half of the physical hub port a device sits on.

    /sys/bus/usb/devices/4-1/4-1:1.0/4-1-port4/peer → …/2-1/2-1:1.0/2-1-port4
    gives ("2-1", "4").  None for hubs without a companion (USB2-only, root).
    """
    hp = split_devpath(devpath)
    if hp is None:
        return None
    hub, port = hp
    link = root / hub / f"{hub}:1.0" / f"{hub}-port{port}" / "peer"
    try:
        target = Path(os.readlink(link)).name          # "2-1-port4"
    except OSError:
        return None
    m = re.fullmatch(r"(.+)-port(\d+)", target)
    return (m.group(1), m.group(2)) if m else None


def _read(p: Path) -> str:
    try:
        return p.read_text().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def all_rx888(root: Path = SYSFS_USB) -> List["Located"]:
    """Every RX-888 on the bus (running 00f1 or FX3 bootloader 00f3), in sysfs
    order.

    ⛔ A STATION MAY HAVE MORE THAN ONE.  N8UR, ON5KQ and KX4AZ-T each run two
    or three cards under WsprDaemon.  The automatic recovery path below still
    watches ONE card (STATE_PATH holds one devpath, and there is no serial →
    radiod@instance map yet), which is a known gap — but the manual --off/--on
    pair must never act on "whichever card sysfs listed first", so it selects
    by serial and refuses an ambiguous station.  See tasks/plan-multi-rx888-sources.md.
    """
    try:
        entries = sorted(root.iterdir())
    except OSError:
        return []
    found: List[Located] = []
    for dev in entries:
        vid_pid = f"{_read(dev / 'idVendor')}:{_read(dev / 'idProduct')}"
        if vid_pid not in RX888_IDS:
            continue
        hp = split_devpath(dev.name)
        if hp is None:
            continue
        peer = peer_of(dev.name, root) or (None, None)
        found.append(Located(dev.name, hp[0], hp[1], _read(dev / "serial"),
                             _read(dev / "speed"), peer[0], peer[1],
                             vid_pid.split(":")[1], _read(dev / "devnum")))
    return found


def locate_rx888(root: Path = SYSFS_USB,
                 serial: Optional[str] = None) -> Optional[Located]:
    """Find the RX-888 (running or in its FX3 bootloader) in sysfs.

    With ``serial``, the card carrying that serial and no other; without it,
    the first — which is what every caller in the automatic path wants and
    has always got.
    """
    cards = all_rx888(root)
    if serial is None:
        return cards[0] if cards else None
    for card in cards:
        if card.serial == serial:
            return card
    return None


SS_SPEED = "5000"          # SuperSpeed, what the RX-888 must have to stream
LINK_BLIP_S = 2            # short enough that the FX3 KEEPS its firmware
# How long --on waits for 00f1 at SuperSpeed.  Longer than the automatic
# path's 90 s because a real VBUS cut adds a whole bootloader + firmware-load
# + re-enumeration cycle before the card is usable (AI6VN measured ~37 s end
# to end on 2026-10-26, and that was a healthy run).
MANUAL_ON_WAIT_S = 120


def link_underspeed(loc: Optional[Located]) -> bool:
    """Is the card present, running its firmware, and stuck below SuperSpeed?

    ⛔ THE FAULT THIS NAMES.  The RX-888 appears in its FX3 bootloader
    (04b4:00f3) at HIGH SPEED, takes its firmware, and must then RE-TRAIN the
    link to SuperSpeed as 04b4:00f1.  Some hubs do not follow that transition
    and leave the card on the USB2 bus at 480 Mb/s, where radiod refuses it:

        rx888_usb_init() failed / device setup returned -1

    and the station looks like it has a dead card while the card sits there,
    enumerated and healthy, on the wrong bus.  Measured on AI6VN 2026-10-21:
    a Lenovo/VIA 17ef:1039 follows it every time; a UGREEN 05e3:0626 never
    does.  A USB-Ethernet adapter never shows it, because it arrives as a USB3
    device and links once -- which is why the hub looks fine under every other
    test (rob diagnosed this from the DFU sequence).

    Only 00f1 counts: the bootloader is EXPECTED at high speed, and calling
    that underspeed would blip the card mid-firmware-load.
    """
    if loc is None or loc.product != "00f1":
        return False
    if (loc.speed or "") in ("", SS_SPEED):
        return False
    # ⛔ ONLY WHERE SUPERSPEED IS PHYSICALLY ON OFFER.  A USB3 hub presents
    # each physical port twice and sysfs gives the other half as `peer`; a
    # card on a genuinely USB2-only path has no peer, so there is no faster
    # link to re-train onto and a blip would drop a working card for nothing.
    # (This is the `usb2-only` case the duality tests already cover.)
    return bool(loc.peer_hub and loc.peer_port)


def promote_link(loc: Located, blip_seconds: int = LINK_BLIP_S) -> Optional[Located]:
    """Blip the card's port BRIEFLY so it re-trains at SuperSpeed.

    ⚡ THE OPPOSITE OF A RECOVERY CYCLE, AND DELIBERATELY SO.  cycle_port()
    uses a LONG cut precisely so the FX3 loses its firmware and reloads --
    which would land the card back in the bootloader at 480 Mb/s, the very
    state we are trying to leave.  Here the cut must be SHORT: this file's own
    note, "the FX3 holds state across a brief drop", is the lever.  The card
    comes back as 00f1 with firmware intact, no DFU phase, and negotiates the
    link afresh.

    Proved on AI6VN 2026-10-21: 2-1.3 speed 480 -> blip -> 4-1.3 speed 5000,
    and radiod started and reported "rx888 running" immediately after.

    Returns the card's new location, or None if it did not come back.
    """
    halves = [(loc.hub, loc.port, False)]
    if loc.peer_hub and loc.peer_port:
        halves.append((loc.peer_hub, loc.peer_port, True))
    _log(f"link is {loc.speed} Mb/s, not {SS_SPEED}: blipping hub {loc.hub} "
         f"port {loc.port} for {blip_seconds}s so it re-trains at SuperSpeed "
         f"(a SHORT cut — a long one would send it back through the bootloader)")
    for hub, port, exact in halves:
        for extra in ([], ["-f"]):
            r = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", "off"]
                     + (["-e"] if exact else []) + extra, timeout=20)
            if r.returncode == 0:
                break
    time.sleep(blip_seconds)
    for hub, port, exact in halves:
        for extra in ([], ["-f"]):
            r = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", "on"]
                     + (["-e"] if exact else []) + extra, timeout=20)
            if r.returncode == 0:
                break
    # The card re-enumerates on the OTHER bus, so re-find it rather than
    # assuming the devpath survived: 2-1.3 became 4-1.3 when it worked.
    for _ in range(12):
        time.sleep(2)
        now = locate_rx888()
        if now is not None and now.product == "00f1":
            if (now.speed or "") == SS_SPEED:
                _log(f"link promoted: {loc.devpath} @ {loc.speed} -> "
                     f"{now.devpath} @ {now.speed} Mb/s")
            return now
    return None


def _uhubctl_sees(hub: str, port: str, exact: bool) -> Optional[bool]:
    """Can uhubctl address this location at all?

    ⛔ FALLS BACK TO --force FOR A *GANGED* HUB.  uhubctl refuses any hub whose
    descriptor says `ganged power switching` rather than per-port, and reports
    the same "No compatible devices detected" it gives for a location that does
    not exist -- so a perfectly usable hub reads as not switchable and the card
    is never recovered.  Measured on AI6VN 2026-10-21: a UGREEN 05e3:0626
    declares ganged, is refused by default, and under -f REALLY drops VBUS --
    a USB-Ethernet adapter vanished from the bus on a single port AND on all
    ports, and returned on restore.  rob's ruling the same day: cutting every
    port at once is acceptable, the other devices re-enumerate.

    ⚠ -f is tried only AFTER the plain form, so a hub that works normally is
    never forced, and the answer still means "uhubctl can drive this", which is
    what the caller asks.  It is NOT a cure for a wrong location: a stale hub
    path fails under -f exactly as it does without it (proved on 4-2 after the
    hub had re-enumerated to 4-1).  That needs the right address, not force.
    """
    for extra in ([], ["-f"]):
        try:
            r = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port]
                     + (["-e"] if exact else []) + extra, timeout=30)
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
            _log(f"uhubctl unavailable: {exc}")
            return None
        seen = parse_uhubctl(r.stdout + "\n" + r.stderr, port)
        if seen:
            if extra:
                _log(f"hub {hub} port {port} is driveable only with --force "
                     f"(its descriptor says ganged, not per-port)")
            return seen
        last = seen
    return last


def live_targets(targets: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]]
                 ) -> Tuple[List[Tuple[str, str, bool]], List[Tuple[str, str, bool]]]:
    """Split targets into (addressable now, stale).

    ⛔ THE FAULT THIS EXISTS FOR.  AI6VN, 2026-10-21: the state recorded the
    card on hub 4-2 port 4.  Across a re-enumeration its hub became 4-1, so
    every cycle ran `uhubctl -l 4-2 -p 4 -a off -e`, got "No compatible devices
    detected at location 4-2", switched a port THAT DID NOT EXIST, saw no card
    return, and entered cooldown -- reporting a failed recovery on a hub that
    was working perfectly.  A plain replug by hand fixed it in seconds.

    A learned bus path is a guess about this boot, not a fact.  Ask uhubctl
    whether each one is still addressable before spending a cycle on it, so a
    stale path is named instead of silently cycling nothing.
    """
    live: List[Tuple[str, str, bool]] = []
    stale: List[Tuple[str, str, bool]] = []
    for hub, port, exact in targets:
        seen = _uhubctl_sees(hub, port, exact)
        if seen is None:                 # uhubctl missing / not root: do not
            return list(targets), []     # second-guess, behave as before
        (live if seen else stale).append((hub, port, exact))
    return live, stale


def hub_switchable(loc: Located) -> Tuple[Optional[bool], List[Tuple[str, str, bool]]]:
    """Ask uhubctl (root) which locations of the card's physical port it can
    switch.  Returns (verdict, locations); every accepted location is kept
    because cycle_port() must hit all of them (see there).  Candidates: the hub
    the card enumerated under, plain then --exact (uhubctl's USB3 duality logic
    rejects some SuperSpeed hubs' own location unless told to be exact), then
    the peer half of the same physical port."""
    candidates = [(loc.hub, loc.port, False), (loc.hub, loc.port, True)]
    if loc.peer_hub and loc.peer_port:
        # The peer plain, then --exact: a card latched on the USB2 hub has the
        # SuperSpeed hub as its peer, and B4's StarTech answers that one only
        # with --exact (`-l 10-1` fails, `-l 10-1 -e` lists the port).
        candidates += [(loc.peer_hub, loc.peer_port, False),
                       (loc.peer_hub, loc.peer_port, True)]
    accepted: List[Tuple[str, str, bool]] = []
    unknown = False
    for hub, port, exact in candidates:
        seen = _uhubctl_sees(hub, port, exact)
        if seen:
            if not any(a[0] == hub and a[1] == port for a in accepted):
                accepted.append((hub, port, exact))
        elif seen is None:
            unknown = True
    if accepted:
        return True, accepted
    return (None if unknown else False), []


def port_claim(cfg: dict) -> str:
    """What the station knows about this port's switching.  A hub's ppps
    claim alone proves nothing (B4's two hubs never cut VBUS), so the default
    is unverified; `[radiod] recover_port_verified = true` records a watched
    cut."""
    if cfg.get("recover_port_verified"):
        return "ppps reported, switching verified (a cut was watched)"
    return ("ppps reported, switching unverified (a port reset only; "
            "the hub may not cut VBUS)")


def hub_speed(hub: str, root: Path = SYSFS_USB) -> Optional[str]:
    """A hub's link speed in Mb/s as sysfs reports it ("480", "5000",
    "10000"), or None.  uhubctl names a root hub by its bus number, and sysfs
    calls that hub "usb<bus>"."""
    name = hub if ("-" in hub or "." in hub) else f"usb{hub}"
    return _read(root / name / "speed") or None


def port_halves(loc: Located, accepted: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]],
                speed_of=None) -> dict:
    """Which halves of the card's physical port uhubctl can switch:
    {"ss": ..., "usb2": ...}, each True (uhubctl accepted a location on that
    half), False (the half exists and uhubctl accepted none of it) or None (no
    such half, or its speed is unreadable).  The halves are the hub the card
    enumerated under and its sysfs peer; a hub at 5000 Mb/s or more is the
    SuperSpeed half.

    A USB2 location uhubctl accepted in the plain form leaves "ss" at None,
    never False: uhubctl's USB3 duality makes a plain `-l <usb2 hub>` switch
    the SuperSpeed companion too.  So the SuperSpeed half reads False only
    when no SuperSpeed location answered, plain or --exact, AND no USB2
    location answered plain."""
    speed_of = speed_of or hub_speed
    kinds = {}
    for h in [loc.hub] + ([loc.peer_hub] if loc.peer_hub else []):
        sp = speed_of(h)
        kinds[h] = (int(sp) >= SUPERSPEED_MBPS) if sp and sp.isdigit() else None
    out = {}
    for half, want in (("ss", True), ("usb2", False)):
        here = [h for h, k in kinds.items() if k is want]
        out[half] = any(a[0] in here for a in accepted) if here else None
    usb2_hubs = [h for h, k in kinds.items() if k is False]
    if out["ss"] is False and any(a[0] in usb2_hubs and not a[2] for a in accepted):
        out["ss"] = None        # reached through duality, as far as uhubctl says
    return out


def port_power(halves: Optional[dict], cfg: dict) -> Tuple[str, str]:
    """What a reset of this port can do: (kind, why).

    "data-only"  the station measured VBUS held through a cut
                 (`[radiod] recover_port_data_only = true`, from a meter or the
                 RX888's LED).  B4's VIA and Anker hubs measured so (10-14).
    "usb2-only"  uhubctl reaches only the USB2 half of the port.  The RX888
                 runs on the SuperSpeed half, so the reset never touches it.
    "both"       a cut was watched (`recover_port_verified = true`).
    "unverified" anything else: uhubctl reaches the port, and nobody has
                 watched a cut.
    """
    if cfg.get("recover_port_data_only"):
        return "data-only", ("the station measured this port: VBUS stays up "
                             "through a cut ([radiod] recover_port_data_only)")
    if (halves or {}).get("ss") is False:
        return "usb2-only", ("uhubctl reaches only the USB2 half of this port, "
                             "and the RX888 runs on the SuperSpeed half")
    if cfg.get("recover_port_verified"):
        return "both", "a cut was watched ([radiod] recover_port_verified)"
    return "unverified", "switching unverified"


# What a port reset can clear, by what the port does (port_power) and what
# went wrong.  A data-only cut still drops the link, which reloads the FX3
# firmware and cleared B4's 10-11 stream wedge; it never cleared a latch or
# brought back a card gone from the bus.
_TRIGGER_WORDS = {"absent": "a card gone from the bus",
                  "latched": "a latched card (no SuperSpeed link, or only the "
                             "FX3 bootloader)",
                  "wedge": "a stream wedge"}


def reset_can_help(power: str, trigger: str) -> bool:
    if power == "usb2-only":
        return False
    if power == "data-only":
        return trigger == "wedge"
    return True


def _devpath(hub: str, port: str) -> str:
    return f"{hub}.{port}" if ("-" in hub or "." in hub) else f"{hub}-{port}"


def port_prefixes(targets: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]],
                  root: Path = SYSFS_USB) -> List[str]:
    """sysfs device names of every target port AND its companion half: a
    plain `uhubctl -a off` also switches the peer half of a USB3 port."""
    out: List[str] = []
    for hub, port, _ in targets:
        dev = _devpath(hub, port)
        names = [dev]
        pr = peer_of(dev, root)
        if pr:
            names.append(_devpath(*pr))
        for name in names:
            if name not in out:
                out.append(name)
    return out


def foreign_devices(targets: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]],
                    root: Path = SYSFS_USB) -> List[Tuple[str, str]]:
    """Devices behind the target hub ports, on either half, that are not the
    RX-888: [(devpath, "vid:pid")].  Anything here forbids the reset, because
    a port reset also drops whatever shares the port (the GPSDO, a TS-1, a
    Pololu board, a downstream hub)."""
    prefixes = port_prefixes(targets, root)
    try:
        entries = sorted(root.iterdir())
    except OSError:
        return []
    found = []
    for dev in entries:
        name = dev.name
        if ":" in name or not any(name == p or name.startswith(p + ".")
                                  for p in prefixes):
            continue
        vid_pid = f"{_read(dev / 'idVendor')}:{_read(dev / 'idProduct')}"
        if vid_pid not in RX888_IDS:
            found.append((name, vid_pid))
    return found


def partition_targets(targets: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]],
                      root: Path = SYSFS_USB):
    """(clean, dirty): targets with only the RX-888 behind them, and
    [(target, [(devpath, id)...])] for those that hold anything else.  Only
    the occupied ports drop out; the rest stay."""
    clean, dirty = [], []
    for t in targets:
        others = foreign_devices([t], root=root)
        (dirty.append((t, others)) if others else clean.append(t))
    return clean, dirty


def kernel_latch_errors(since: float,
                        prefixes: Optional[Sequence[str]] = None) -> List[str]:
    """Kernel lines since ``since`` (epoch) carrying the cold-start latch's
    signature, -71 or -110.  With ``prefixes`` (the card's own port names,
    both halves) only lines naming those ports count: a -71 on the GPSDO's
    port must not mark the card latched.

    ⛔ --since takes "@<epoch>": a date string is read as LOCAL time even with
    --utc (systemd 257: one string gave 4 lines under UTC, 0 under Chicago)."""
    r = _run(["journalctl", "-k", "--no-pager", "-o", "cat",
              "--since", f"@{int(since)}"], timeout=30)
    out = []
    for l in (r.stdout or "").splitlines():
        if not LATCH_RE.search(l):
            continue
        if prefixes is not None and not any(
                re.search(rf"\busb {re.escape(p)}[:.\s]", l) for p in prefixes):
            continue
        out.append(l)
    return out


def classify_return(card: Optional[Located],
                    kernel_errors: Sequence[str]) -> Tuple[str, str]:
    """How the card came back from a port reset: (verdict, why).

    "recovered"  00f1 on a SuperSpeed path and no -71/-110 since the reset.
    "latched"    on the USB2 half only, or the kernel reported -71/-110, or
                 only its FX3 bootloader (00f3) shows.  04b4:00f3 proves
                 nothing about power or health.
    "never-back" not on the bus."""
    if card is None:
        return "never-back", "the card did not return to the bus"
    if kernel_errors:
        return "latched", f"the kernel reported {kernel_errors[0].strip()[:80]}"
    if card.product != "00f1":
        return "latched", (f"only the FX3 bootloader ({card.product or '?'}) "
                           f"shows; the firmware never started")
    if card.speed.isdigit() and int(card.speed) < SUPERSPEED_MBPS:
        return "latched", (f"the card is on the USB2 half only ({card.speed} "
                           f"Mb/s), with no SuperSpeed link")
    return "recovered", "00f1 on a SuperSpeed path"


def radiod_wedge_signals(unit: str, now: float,
                         since: Optional[float] = None) -> Tuple[int, int]:
    """(stall lines, restart/abort lines) in radiod's journal from ``since``
    (default: the last WEDGE_WINDOW_S)."""
    start = now - WEDGE_WINDOW_S if since is None else since
    r = _run(["journalctl", "-u", unit, "--no-pager", "-o", "cat",
              "--since", f"@{int(start)}"], timeout=30)
    lines = (r.stdout or "").splitlines()
    return (sum(1 for l in lines if STALL_RE.search(l)),
            sum(1 for l in lines if EXIT_RE.search(l)))


def sample_nrestarts(unit: str, state: dict, now: float) -> int:
    """NRestarts growth since the previous tick's sample, 0 when there is no
    sample or it is older than NRESTART_SAMPLE_MAX_AGE_S.  Called every tick,
    card present or not, so a delta is never a stale one."""
    r = _run(["systemctl", "show", "-p", "NRestarts", "--value", unit], timeout=15)
    try:
        n = int((r.stdout or "").strip())
    except ValueError:
        return 0
    prev = state.get("nrestarts_sample")
    state["nrestarts_sample"] = [now, n]
    if prev and now - prev[0] <= NRESTART_SAMPLE_MAX_AGE_S:
        return max(0, n - prev[1])
    return 0


def is_wedged(stall_hits: int, exit_hits: int, restarts_delta: int,
              usb2: bool = False) -> bool:
    """radiod restart-looping WITH USB stall lines: enough stall lines AND
    evidence of restarts (an abort/restart line, or NRestarts growing).
    Neither alone is a wedge."""
    need = 1 if usb2 else WEDGE_MIN_HITS     # a card below SuperSpeed needs one
    return stall_hits >= need and (exit_hits >= 1 or restarts_delta >= 1)


def radiod_is_wedged(unit: Optional[str], state: dict, now: float,
                     card: Optional[Located] = None) -> str:
    """"" or the trigger, in words.  A radiod that fails inside the boot
    grace is the known RX888 firmware race, not a wedge.  The journal is read
    from the latest of: 10 minutes ago, the last reset, the card's last
    return -- lines from before those cannot re-trigger or delay a clear."""
    # The lowered USB2 threshold applies only when the SAME enumeration of the
    # card was below SuperSpeed on the previous tick too: a healthy card is
    # briefly 00f3 at 480 Mb/s while it re-enumerates.
    below = bool(card is not None and card.speed.isdigit()
                 and int(card.speed) < SUPERSPEED_MBPS)
    key = f"{card.devpath}/{card.devnum}" if card is not None else None
    usb2 = below and state.get("usb2_key") == key
    state["usb2_key"] = key if below else None
    if not unit:
        return ""
    up = sdr_events.uptime_s()
    if up is not None and up < sdr_events.BOOT_GRACE_S:
        return ""
    delta = sample_nrestarts(unit, state, now)
    floor = max(now - WEDGE_WINDOW_S, float(state.get("last_cycle_at") or 0),
                float(state.get("card_return_at") or 0))
    stall, exits = radiod_wedge_signals(unit, now, since=floor)
    if not is_wedged(stall, exits, delta, usb2=usb2):
        return ""
    return (f"{stall} USB stall lines in {WEDGE_WINDOW_S // 60} min with "
            f"{exits} abort/restart lines and NRestarts +{delta}")


def detect_radiod_unit() -> Optional[str]:
    """The radiod@ instance to restart, when [radiod].unit is not declared.

    ⛔ MUST NOT REQUIRE AN *ACTIVE* INSTANCE.  This helper exists for the case
    where the card has vanished — and radiod cannot serve without the card, so
    it is never active then: it sits in activating/auto-restart, or failed.
    Filtering on --state=active therefore made the helper refuse to run in
    precisely the situation it was written for.

    AI6VN, 2026-09-29: the RX-888 vanished, radiod@AI6VN went to
    auto-restart with NRestarts=15, and every single run of this helper logged
    "not exactly one active radiod@ instance — set it in topology.toml" and
    exited 0.  The card's hub and port were already learned and persisted, the
    hub supports per-port power switching, uhubctl was installed — and nothing
    happened, once a minute, indefinitely.

    So: prefer a single ACTIVE instance (unambiguous), and fall back to a
    single LOADED instance whatever its state.  Two or more is still ambiguous
    and still returns None — guessing which radiod to restart is worse than
    asking topology.toml to say.
    """
    for extra in (["--state=active"], ["--all"]):
        out = _run(["systemctl", "list-units", "--no-legend", "--plain",
                    *extra, "radiod@*.service"]).stdout
        units = [l.split()[0] for l in out.splitlines() if l.strip()]
        if len(units) == 1:
            return units[0]
    return None


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Decision:
    cycle: bool
    reason: str


def should_cycle(
    device_present: bool,
    absent_for_s: float,
    last_cycle_age_s: Optional[float],
) -> Decision:
    """Whether to reset the port now, and why — or why not.

    Returns the reason either way: a watchdog that cannot say why it stayed
    its hand is as hard to trust as one that cannot say why it acted.
    """
    if device_present:
        return Decision(False, "device present on the bus — nothing to recover")
    if absent_for_s < ABSENT_GRACE_S:
        return Decision(
            False,
            f"absent {absent_for_s:.0f}s — inside the {ABSENT_GRACE_S}s grace "
            f"window, a re-enumeration looks like this",
        )
    if last_cycle_age_s is not None and last_cycle_age_s < CYCLE_COOLDOWN_S:
        return Decision(
            False,
            f"cooldown — cycled {last_cycle_age_s:.0f}s ago, holding off until "
            f"{CYCLE_COOLDOWN_S}s so a failed recovery cannot become a loop",
        )
    return Decision(True, f"device absent {absent_for_s:.0f}s — resetting its hub port")


def expand_consumers(
    patterns: Sequence[str], installed: Iterable[str]
) -> List[str]:
    """Declared unit patterns → actual units, in the declared order."""
    return expand_consumers_checked(patterns, installed)[0]


def expand_consumers_checked(
    patterns: Sequence[str], installed: Iterable[str]
) -> Tuple[List[str], List[str]]:
    """As above, plus the patterns that matched nothing.

    A pattern matching nothing means a consumer was renamed, disabled, or
    never installed.  Restarting fewer units than declared, silently, is
    exactly the failure this module exists to prevent — so the caller is
    handed the misses and is expected to say so out loud.
    """
    units = list(installed)
    matched: List[str] = []
    missing: List[str] = []
    for pattern in patterns:
        hits = [u for u in units if fnmatch.fnmatch(u, pattern)]
        if not hits:
            missing.append(pattern)
            continue
        for hit in hits:
            if hit not in matched:
                matched.append(hit)
    return matched, missing


# ─── everything below touches the system; the logic above does not ───────────

def _log(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"sigmond-sdr-recover: {msg}", flush=True)


def _run(cmd: Sequence[str], timeout: int = 60) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """Run and never raise on a slow child: a timeout becomes rc=124 with a log
    line.  An uncaught TimeoutExpired mid-recovery aborted the whole run twice
    on AI6VN (2026-09-05/06), leaving consumers on stale RTP anchors — the
    exact silent failure this helper exists to prevent."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        _log(f"timed out after {timeout}s: {' '.join(cmd)}")
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(list(cmd), 124, "", f"timeout {timeout}s")


def device_present() -> bool:
    out = _run(["lsusb"]).stdout
    return any(dev_id in out for dev_id in RX888_IDS)


def installed_units() -> List[str]:
    out = _run(["systemctl", "list-units", "--all", "--no-legend",
                "--plain", "--type=service"]).stdout
    return [line.split()[0] for line in out.splitlines() if line.strip()]


def load_state() -> dict:
    try:
        return json.loads(STATE_PATH.read_text())
    except Exception:
        return {}


def save_state(state: dict) -> None:
    try:
        STATE_PATH.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        STATE_PATH.write_text(json.dumps(state))
    except Exception as exc:            # never fail the run over bookkeeping
        _log(f"could not persist state: {exc}")


def note_event(kind: str, detail: str) -> None:
    """One line per episode in /var/lib/sigmond/sdr-events.jsonl, which
    `smd doctor` reads.  Bookkeeping never fails a recovery."""
    try:
        sdr_events.record(kind, detail, now=time.time())
    except Exception as exc:            # noqa: BLE001
        _log(f"could not record the {kind}: {exc}")


def log_on_change(state: dict, msg: str) -> None:
    """Log `msg` only when it says something new.  The timer runs this helper
    every 30 s, and a card that stays away used to log "holding: ..." on
    every tick.  Numbers carry no news ("absent 45s" and "absent 75s" say the
    same thing), so the comparison blanks them.  The key lives in the state
    file; the card's return and each port reset clear it."""
    key = re.sub(r"\d+", "#", msg)
    if state.get("said") != key:
        _log(msg)
        state["said"] = key


def read_config() -> dict:
    try:
        import tomllib
        with CONFIG_PATH.open("rb") as fh:
            return tomllib.load(fh).get("radiod", {})
    except Exception as exc:
        _log(f"could not read {CONFIG_PATH}: {exc}")
        return {}


_HUB_LINE = re.compile(r"^Current status for hub (\S+)\s*\[(.*)\]\s*$")
_PORT_LINE = re.compile(r"^\s*Port (\d+):\s*[0-9a-fA-F]{4}\s*(.*)$")


def bootstrap_locations() -> List[Tuple[str, str, bool]]:
    """Ports to reset when the card has NEVER been seen, so nothing was learned.

    A latched RX-888 shows up in uhubctl as a port with power (often with the
    connect bit set, meaning something IS plugged in) and an empty device
    bracket -- nothing enumerated.  An empty port cannot belong to a working
    peripheral, so resetting every device-less port on every switchable hub
    reaches the card without touching anything that works.  Both halves of a
    USB3 dock qualify naturally: the SuperSpeed half lists its ports empty too,
    and cycle_port() switches all locations off before any back on.
    """
    try:
        r = _run([UHUBCTL], timeout=30)
    except Exception as exc:                       # uhubctl missing / not root
        _log(f"bootstrap: uhubctl unavailable: {exc}")
        return []
    found: List[Tuple[str, str, bool]] = []
    occupied: set = set()          # (hub, port) that hold a device
    hub: Optional[str] = None
    switchable = False
    for line in (r.stdout + "\n" + r.stderr).splitlines():
        m = _HUB_LINE.match(line.strip())
        if m:
            hub, switchable = m.group(1), ("ppps" in m.group(2))
            continue
        m = _PORT_LINE.match(line)
        if not (m and hub and switchable):
            continue
        dev = re.search(r"\[([^\]]*)\]", m.group(2))
        if dev and dev.group(1).strip():           # a real device -- never touch
            occupied.add((hub, m.group(1)))
            continue
        found.append((hub, m.group(1), False))

    # ⛔ A PORT IS NOT EMPTY JUST BECAUSE *THIS* HALF OF IT IS.
    # A USB3 hub presents each physical port twice, and a USB2-only device
    # shows up on the SuperSpeed half as an empty port -- while sharing that
    # port's VBUS.  Cutting the "empty" half therefore cuts the device.
    #
    # AI6VN, 2026-09-29: recovering the RX-888 cut 5-1 ports 1, 2 and 3 because
    # they listed empty, and took the GPSDO, the TS-1 and the magnetometer down
    # with them -- they live on physical ports 1-3 and enumerate on the USB2
    # half (3-1).  They came back after the ~6 s cut, but knocking a GPSDO off
    # its lock on every recovery is not acceptable collateral.
    #
    # The kernel already knows the pairing: /sys/.../<hub>-port<N>/peer.  Use
    # it, and drop any candidate whose peer half is occupied.
    paired: List[Tuple[str, str, bool]] = []
    for hub_c, port_c, exact_c in found:
        pr = peer_of(f"{hub_c}.{port_c}")
        if pr and pr in occupied:
            _log(f"not cycling hub {hub_c} port {port_c}: its peer "
                 f"{pr[0]} port {pr[1]} has a device, and both halves share "
                 f"VBUS — resetting it would drop that device too")
            continue
        paired.append((hub_c, port_c, exact_c))
    found = paired
    # Address each hub the way uhubctl accepts it.  A USB3 hub is often
    # reachable ONLY with --exact ("No compatible devices detected at location
    # 4-1!" for the plain form on AI6VN's VIA dock), and a cycle whose commands
    # all fail leaves the card latched while looking like it ran.
    verified: List[Tuple[str, str, bool]] = []
    for hub_l, port_l, _ in found:
        for exact in (False, True):
            if _uhubctl_sees(hub_l, port_l, exact):
                verified.append((hub_l, port_l, exact))
                break
    return verified


def cycle_port(locations: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]], off_seconds: int) -> bool:
    """Switch every known location of the physical port off, then on: a port
    reset.  Whether the hub also drops VBUS is unverified unless the station
    says a cut was watched (B4's hubs drop only the data link).

    A short off is not enough: the FX3 holds state across a brief drop.  A
    long enough off makes the card reload its firmware and re-enumerate,
    usually through its bootloader (04b4:00f3) and then 00f1.  Seeing 00f3 on
    the way back proves nothing about power.

    Every location, not the first that answers: a USB3 hub exposes the same
    physical port twice, and which half's power bit actually drops VBUS is the
    hub's business.  On AI6VN's VIA VL817 dock (2026-09-05) the USB2 half
    reported "off" while the card streamed on regardless, and only the
    SuperSpeed half addressed with --exact cut it.  Powering off all halves,
    then on, is right whichever half is wired to the switch.
    """
    ok = True
    # ⛔ ONLY the locations we actually switched OFF may be reported as left
    # dark.  Bus paths renumber across boots -- AI6VN saw three different
    # numberings in three boots -- so a stale location routinely names a hub
    # that does not exist this boot.  uhubctl then says "No compatible devices
    # detected at location 4-1", nothing was powered off there, and there is
    # nothing to leave off.  Treating that as PORT LEFT OFF sent rob to the
    # bench to look at a card that was powered and streaming (2026-09-29).
    powered_off: List[Tuple[str, str, bool]] = []
    for hub, port, exact in locations:
        _log(f"port reset: switching hub {hub} port {port} off"
             f"{' (exact)' if exact else ''}")
        r = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", "off"]
                 + (["-e"] if exact else []), timeout=20)
        # A GANGED hub is refused by default with the same words uhubctl uses
        # for a location that does not exist, so retry under --force before
        # concluding anything.  See _uhubctl_sees() for the measurement.
        if r.returncode != 0 and "no compatible devices" in (r.stderr or "").lower():
            r = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", "off"]
                     + (["-e"] if exact else []) + ["-f"], timeout=20)
            if r.returncode == 0:
                _log(f"hub {hub} port {port}: switched off only under --force "
                     f"(ganged hub)")
        if r.returncode != 0:
            missing = "no compatible devices" in (r.stderr or "").lower()
            _log(f"uhubctl off failed rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()}"
                 + (" — that hub is not on this bus, so nothing was switched"
                    " off there" if missing else ""))
            # A hub that is not here is not a failure of THIS cycle: the union
            # with every device-less port is what finds the card now.
            if not missing:
                ok = False
            continue
        powered_off.append((hub, port, exact))
    time.sleep(off_seconds)
    for hub, port, exact in powered_off:
        r = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", "on"]
                 + (["-e"] if exact else []), timeout=20)
        # ⛔ THE ON PATH MUST TRY HARDER THAN THE OFF PATH: a port left dark
        # needs a human at the machine.  Force first, then the other
        # addressing below.
        if r.returncode != 0:
            rf = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", "on"]
                      + (["-e"] if exact else []) + ["-f"], timeout=20)
            if rf.returncode == 0:
                _log(f"hub {hub} port {port}: switched back on under --force")
                r = rf
        if r.returncode != 0:
            # ⛔ A FAILED "on" LEAVES THE PORT DEAD.  That is strictly worse
            # than never having cycled: the card cannot come back until a human
            # reaches the hub.  On AI6VN the --exact form of "on" HUNG and was
            # killed at 60 s (rc=124) while the plain form returned instantly,
            # so always try the other addressing before giving up.
            _log(f"uhubctl on failed rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()} "
                 f"— retrying the other addressing so the port is not left off")
            r2 = _run([UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", "on"]
                      + ([] if exact else ["-e"]), timeout=20)
            if r2.returncode != 0:
                _log(f"PORT LEFT OFF on hub {hub} port {port} — both forms "
                     f"failed (rc={r2.returncode}); this needs a human")
                ok = False
            else:
                _log(f"port switched back on, hub {hub} port {port}, via the "
                     f"{'plain' if exact else 'exact'} form")
    return ok


def wait_for_device(timeout_s: int = 90) -> bool:
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout_s
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        if device_present():
            return True
        time.sleep(2)
    return False


# final review / I-3: `smd admin radiod restart-stale-consumers` runs on
# every radiod start and restarts the same consumers this helper does, under
# smd's own lifecycle lock.  Without sharing that lock the two can race and
# double-restart a consumer.  Retry, never fail: a vanished RX-888 must still
# be recovered even if smd happens to be mid-operation.
LIFECYCLE_LOCK_WAIT_S = 600
LIFECYCLE_LOCK_POLL_S = 5


@contextlib.contextmanager
def _with_lifecycle_lock():
    """Hold the same lifecycle lock `smd` uses (LIFECYCLE_LOCK, LOCK_EX) for
    the duration of the `with` block. Waits up to LIFECYCLE_LOCK_WAIT_S,
    polling every LIFECYCLE_LOCK_POLL_S. Still held at the deadline: log
    loudly and proceed WITHOUT it — a vanished card must still be recovered
    even if smd is mid-operation."""
    try:
        LIFECYCLE_LOCK.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        fd = os.open(str(LIFECYCLE_LOCK), os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o644)
    except OSError as exc:
        _log(f"could not open the lifecycle lock ({exc}) — proceeding without it")
        yield
        return
    deadline = time.monotonic() + LIFECYCLE_LOCK_WAIT_S
    held = False
    try:
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                held = True
                break
            except OSError:
                if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                    _log(f"lifecycle lock still held after {LIFECYCLE_LOCK_WAIT_S}s — "
                         "proceeding WITHOUT it (a vanished card must still be recovered)")
                    break
                time.sleep(LIFECYCLE_LOCK_POLL_S)
        yield
    finally:
        if held:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        os.close(fd)


def _unit_started_mono(unit: str) -> Optional[float]:
    """Seconds since boot at which ``unit`` last entered active (monotonic, so
    a host clock step cannot confuse it); None unless the unit is active NOW.
    systemd keeps ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic after a unit stops, so the
    state must be read too."""
    r = _run(["systemctl", "show", "-p", "ActiveState",
              "-p", "ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic", unit], timeout=15)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    props = dict(l.split("=", 1) for l in (r.stdout or "").splitlines() if "=" in l)
    if props.get("ActiveState", "").strip() != "active":
        return None
    try:
        us = int(props.get("ActiveEnterTimestampMonotonic", ""))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return us / 1e6 if us > 0 else None


def restart_station(radiod_unit: str, consumers: Sequence[str],
                    ready_timeout_s: int = 90, consumer_timeout_s: int = 240) -> bool:
    """radiod first, then its consumers — the only order that works."""
    _log(f"starting {radiod_unit}")
    _run(["systemctl", "reset-failed", radiod_unit])
    if _run(["systemctl", "restart", radiod_unit]).returncode != 0:
        _log(f"{radiod_unit} failed to start — leaving consumers alone, since "
             f"restarting them against a dead radiod only spreads the fault")
        return False
    # The subprocess timeout must outlast the wait we ask for, or a slow
    # radiod start becomes an uncaught TimeoutExpired and the consumers are
    # never restarted (AI6VN 2026-09-05: 90 s asked, 60 s default, traceback).
    ready = _run(["/usr/local/sbin/sigmond-radiod-ready", str(ready_timeout_s)],
                 timeout=ready_timeout_s + 30)
    if ready.returncode != 0:
        _log("radiod did not report ready in time — consumers NOT restarted")
        return False
    # Queue every consumer restart without waiting: a recorder can take a
    # minute to stop and another to start (wspr-recorder@AI6VN: ~100 s), and
    # waiting on each in turn is what let one slow unit starve the rest.
    radiod_started = _unit_started_mono(radiod_unit)
    for unit in consumers:
        started = _unit_started_mono(unit)
        if (radiod_started is not None and started is not None
                and started > radiod_started + READY_SETTLE_S):
            _log(f"consumer {unit} already fresh (started after {radiod_unit}) — not restarting")
            continue
        _log(f"restarting consumer {unit}")
        _run(["systemctl", "reset-failed", unit])
        r = _run(["systemctl", "restart", "--no-block", unit])
        if r.returncode != 0:
            _log(f"restart of {unit} not accepted rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()}")
    # Then wait, bounded, for the whole set; name whatever is still not up.
    deadline = time.monotonic() + consumer_timeout_s
    pending = list(consumers)
    while pending and time.monotonic() < deadline:
        time.sleep(5)
        pending = [u for u in pending
                   if _run(["systemctl", "is-active", u]).stdout.strip() != "active"]
    for unit in pending:
        _log(f"consumer {unit} still not active after {consumer_timeout_s}s — "
             f"check: systemctl status {unit}")
    return not pending


def _raise_replug_alarm(state: dict, now: float, why: str,
                        tried: bool = True) -> None:
    """One alarm per episode, never per tick: the sdr-events line `smd doctor`
    and the heartbeat read, then a latch that stops further resets until the
    card returns healthy.  ``tried`` False: no reset ran, because none could
    clear this fault on this port (reset_can_help)."""
    state["latched"] = True
    if not state.get("replug_alarm_at"):
        state["replug_alarm_at"] = now
        if tried:
            _log(f"ALARM: the RX888 needs a hand replug at the RX888 — a port "
                 f"reset did not clear it ({why}); not resetting again until "
                 f"it is back")
        else:
            _log(f"ALARM: the RX888 needs a hand replug at the RX888 — {why}; "
                 f"no port reset tried")
        note_event("needs-replug", why)
    save_state(state)


def _power_now(state: dict, cfg: dict) -> Tuple[str, str]:
    """port_power for this run.  The halves come from what the learn step
    recorded; an explicit [radiod] recover_hub has none, so only the
    station's own flags speak for it."""
    halves = {} if cfg.get("recover_hub") else state.get("halves")
    return port_power(halves, cfg)


# ─── manual power control: --off / --on ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Why this exists.  The only proof that a hub really cuts VBUS is watching the
# RX-888's LED go dark, and an automatic 5-25 s reset is not watchable: by the
# time you look up the card is back.  rob needs a pair of commands that take
# the radio down and LEAVE it down, so the hub can be judged by eye, the cable
# swapped, or the card moved -- the same job WsprDaemon's `wd -U off` / `-U on`
# do (wd-usb-power.sh, commit b0c142c).
#
# What makes it more than two uhubctl calls:
#   * both halves of a USB3 jack must go off, and the card must be confirmed
#     gone from the bus afterwards, or "off" is a claim and not a measurement;
#   * radiod must stop FIRST, or it spends the outage logging USB errors and
#     re-exec'ing, and its consumers sit on a stale RTP anchor;
#   * the automatic watchdogs must be told to stand down, or the next timer
#     tick "recovers" the radio the operator just switched off.
#
# The hold file is that stand-down.  It is deliberately a FILE and not a
# stopped timer: a stopped timer is invisible a week later and survives no
# reboot policy, whereas `--status` reads the hold and says who holds what and
# since when.


def load_hold() -> dict:
    try:
        data = json.loads(HOLD_PATH.read_text())
    except Exception:
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def save_hold(holds: dict) -> None:
    try:
        HOLD_PATH.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        if holds:
            HOLD_PATH.write_text(json.dumps(holds, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        elif HOLD_PATH.exists():
            HOLD_PATH.unlink()
    except OSError as exc:
        _log(f"could not write {HOLD_PATH}: {exc}")


def held_serials() -> List[str]:
    return sorted(load_hold().keys())


def _ss_first(locations: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]]
              ) -> List[Tuple[str, str, bool]]:
    """SuperSpeed half first.  Switching the SS half on first is what lets the
    FX3 train at 5 Gb/s on the way back; bringing the USB2 half up first can
    leave the card enumerated at 480 and radiod refusing it."""
    def rank(loc):
        sp = hub_speed(loc[0])
        return 0 if (sp and sp.isdigit() and int(sp) >= SUPERSPEED_MBPS) else 1
    return sorted(locations, key=rank)


def _uhubctl_set(hub: str, port: str, exact: bool, action: str) -> bool:
    """One uhubctl action, with the two retries the automatic path learned:
    --force for a ganged hub, and the other addressing when the first form
    fails or hangs.  Returns whether the port ended up in the asked state."""
    base = [UHUBCTL, "-l", hub, "-p", port, "-a", action]
    forms = [base + (["-e"] if exact else []),
             base + (["-e"] if exact else []) + ["-f"],
             base + ([] if exact else ["-e"]),
             base + ([] if exact else ["-e"]) + ["-f"]]
    for i, cmd in enumerate(forms):
        r = _run(cmd, timeout=20)
        if r.returncode == 0:
            if i:
                _log(f"hub {hub} port {port}: {action} accepted on attempt "
                     f"{i + 1} ({' '.join(cmd[5:]) or 'plain'})")
            return True
        if "no compatible devices" not in (r.stderr or "").lower() and i >= 2:
            _log(f"uhubctl {action} failed rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()}")
    return False


def _stop_station(radiod_unit: Optional[str], consumers: Sequence[str]) -> None:
    """Consumers down first, then radiod -- the reverse of restart_station()."""
    for unit in reversed(list(consumers)):
        _log(f"stopping consumer {unit}")
        _run(["systemctl", "stop", unit], timeout=180)
    if radiod_unit:
        _log(f"stopping {radiod_unit}")
        _run(["systemctl", "stop", radiod_unit], timeout=180)


def _select_card(serial: Optional[str]) -> Tuple[Optional[Located], Optional[str]]:
    """(card, error).  Refuses to guess on a multi-card station."""
    cards = all_rx888()
    if not cards:
        return None, "no RX-888 is on the bus — nothing to switch off"
    if serial:
        for card in cards:
            if card.serial == serial:
                return card, None
        return None, (f"no RX-888 with serial {serial} on the bus; present: "
                      + ", ".join(c.serial or "(no serial)" for c in cards))
    if len(cards) > 1:
        return None, ("this station has " + str(len(cards)) + " RX-888s ("
                      + ", ".join(f"{c.serial or '(no serial)'} on {c.devpath}"
                                  for c in cards)
                      + ") — name one with --serial; guessing which radio to "
                        "power down is not something this command will do")
    return cards[0], None


def unit_for_serial(serial: Optional[str]) -> Optional[str]:
    """`radiod@<id>.service` for the radiod whose conf (or coordination.toml)
    names this card's serial; None when no map names it or it cannot be read.
    On a multi-card host this outranks [radiod].unit: the designated unit is
    one radiod, and the card being held may belong to another."""
    if not serial:
        return None
    try:
        from sigmond import sdr_map
        inst = sdr_map.serial_to_instance(sdr_map.instance_serials()).get(
            str(serial).strip().upper())
    except Exception:
        return None
    return f"radiod@{inst}.service" if inst else None


def manual_off(serial: Optional[str], cfg: dict) -> int:
    """Take one RX-888's port down and LEAVE it down, under a hold."""
    card, err = _select_card(serial)
    if err or card is None:
        _log(err or "could not locate the card")
        return 1
    key = card.serial or card.devpath
    holds = load_hold()
    if key in holds:
        _log(f"{key} is already held off since "
             f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime(holds[key].get('since', 0)))}"
             f" — run --on first")
        return 1

    # 1. Every half of the physical jack must be switchable, or "off" is a
    #    half-measure: the card keeps VBUS from the half uhubctl could not
    #    reach and never leaves the bus.
    sw, accepted = hub_switchable(card)
    if not accepted:
        _log(f"uhubctl cannot switch the port {card.devpath} sits on "
             f"(hub {card.hub} port {card.port}"
             + (f", peer {card.peer_hub} port {card.peer_port}" if card.peer_hub else "")
             + ") — this hub has no per-port power switching, so there is "
               "nothing to turn off")
        return 1
    halves = port_halves(card, accepted)
    unreachable = [h for h, ok in halves.items() if ok is False]
    if unreachable:
        _log(f"REFUSING: uhubctl does not accept the {', '.join(unreachable)} "
             f"half of this jack, and both halves share VBUS — switching only "
             f"one leaves the card powered while reporting success")
        return 1

    # 2. Nothing but the RX-888 may share the jack, either half.
    clean, dirty = partition_targets(accepted)
    if dirty:
        _log("REFUSING: " + ", ".join(
            f"hub {h} port {p} also holds " + ", ".join(f"{d} ({i})" for d, i in o)
            for (h, p, _), o in dirty)
            + " — a power cut would drop it too")
        return 1

    radiod_unit = (unit_for_serial(card.serial) or cfg.get("unit")
                   or detect_radiod_unit())
    consumers, missing = expand_consumers_checked(cfg.get("consumers", []),
                                                  installed_units())
    if missing:
        _log(f"declared consumers that match no installed unit: {missing}")
    if not consumers:
        _log("NO CONSUMERS DECLARED in [radiod].consumers — only radiod will "
             "be stopped and restarted; recorders keep a stale RTP anchor "
             "until restarted by hand.  Declare them in topology.toml.")

    power, why = port_power(halves, cfg)
    # 3. Write the hold BEFORE touching anything, so a timer that fires while
    #    the units are stopping finds the hold and stands down.
    holds[key] = {
        "serial": card.serial, "devpath": card.devpath,
        "locations": [list(a) for a in clean],
        "radiod_unit": radiod_unit, "consumers": consumers,
        "since": time.time(), "port_power": power,
    }
    save_hold(holds)
    _log(f"hold written for {key}: the recovery timer and the radiod watchdog "
         f"will leave this radio alone until --on")

    with _with_lifecycle_lock():
        _stop_station(radiod_unit, consumers)
        ok = True
        for hub, port, exact in clean:
            _log(f"switching hub {hub} port {port} OFF"
                 f"{' (exact)' if exact else ''}")
            if not _uhubctl_set(hub, port, exact, "off"):
                _log(f"uhubctl could not switch hub {hub} port {port} off")
                ok = False

    # 4. "Off" is a measurement, not a claim: the card must leave the bus.
    gone = False
    for _ in range(10):
        time.sleep(1)
        if locate_rx888(serial=card.serial) is None:
            gone = True
            break
    if not gone:
        _log(f"⛔ {card.serial or card.devpath} is STILL on the bus after the "
             f"port was switched off — the hold stands and the ports are off, "
             f"so run --on to restore, but do not trust this hub's switching")
        return 1
    if not ok:
        _log("⚠ at least one uhubctl off failed, though the card did leave "
             "the bus — read the lines above before trusting this port")
    _log(f"{card.serial or card.devpath} is off the bus and its port is held "
         f"off.  Port switching is {power}: {why}.")
    _log("Look at the RX-888's LED now: dark means this hub really cuts VBUS; "
         "still lit means it dropped only the data link.  "
         "Restore with: sigmond-sdr-recover --on"
         + (f" --serial {card.serial}" if card.serial else ""))
    return 0


def wait_for_running_card(locations: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, bool]],
                          serial: Optional[str],
                          timeout_s: int = 120,
                          blip_seconds: int = LINK_BLIP_S
                          ) -> Tuple[Optional[Located], str]:
    """Wait for the card to be READY — 04b4:00f1 at SuperSpeed — not merely
    on the bus.  Returns (card, why): card is None if it never got there.

    ⛔ "ON THE BUS" IS NOT "READY", AND THE DIFFERENCE COST US A FAILED START.
    Measured on AI6VN 2026-10-26 21:42:49Z: after a real VBUS cut the card
    comes back as its FX3 BOOTLOADER, 04b4:00f3 at 480 Mb/s, on the USB2 half
    (3-1.4).  `rx888_boot` then loads the firmware and the card re-enumerates
    as 04b4:00f1 at 5000 on the SuperSpeed half (4-1.4) a few seconds later.
    An --on that took the first sighting as "back" started radiod against the
    bootloader; radiod exited status=66/NOINPUT and only recovered because
    systemd's Restart= tried again 11 s later.  A station without Restart=,
    or one where the retry also lands early, is simply down.

    So the bootloader is an EXPECTED intermediate state and is waited through,
    not treated as arrival.  The three terminal answers are: ready; present
    but stuck (00f3 that never promoted, or 00f1 the hub never re-trained
    past 480); or never seen at all.  Each is reported in its own words,
    because the operator's next move differs for each.

    Identity: the card is matched by (hub, port) against the ports --off
    switched, and by serial.  The hub/port test is what carries the match
    through the bootloader phase, where the FX3 need not present the serial
    the running firmware does.
    """
    jacks = {(h, p) for h, p, _ in locations}

    def candidate() -> Optional[Located]:
        best = None
        for card in all_rx888():
            if serial and card.serial == serial:
                return card
            if (card.hub, card.port) in jacks:
                best = best or card
        return best

    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout_s
    seen = None
    blipped = False
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        card = candidate()
        if card is not None:
            seen = card
            if card.product == "00f1" and (card.speed or "") == SS_SPEED:
                return card, "ready"
            if link_underspeed(card) and not blipped:
                # 00f1 but the hub did not follow the bootloader-to-SuperSpeed
                # transition.  One short blip re-trains it; see promote_link.
                _log(f"card returned at {card.speed} Mb/s, not {SS_SPEED} — "
                     f"blipping the link to promote it")
                blipped = True
                promoted = promote_link(card, blip_seconds)
                if promoted is not None:
                    seen = promoted
                    if (promoted.speed or "") == SS_SPEED:
                        return promoted, "ready after a link blip"
        time.sleep(2)

    if seen is None:
        return None, (f"the card never returned to the bus within {timeout_s}s")
    if seen.product == "00f3":
        return None, (f"the card is stuck in its FX3 bootloader "
                      f"(04b4:00f3 at {seen.speed} Mb/s on {seen.devpath}) "
                      f"after {timeout_s}s — the firmware loader did not "
                      f"finish; check rx888_boot and its udev rule")
    return None, (f"the card is on the bus as 04b4:{seen.product} at "
                  f"{seen.speed} Mb/s, not {SS_SPEED}, after {timeout_s}s — "
                  f"this hub does not follow the bootloader-to-SuperSpeed "
                  f"transition; radiod would refuse the card")


def manual_on(serial: Optional[str], cfg: dict,
              wait_seconds: int = MANUAL_ON_WAIT_S) -> int:
    """Restore a port taken down by --off, then bring the station back."""
    holds = load_hold()
    if not holds:
        _log(f"no hold in {HOLD_PATH} — nothing is held off by this command")
        return 1
    if serial:
        keys = [k for k in holds if k == serial or holds[k].get("serial") == serial]
        if not keys:
            _log(f"no hold for serial {serial}; held: {sorted(holds)}")
            return 1
    elif len(holds) > 1:
        _log(f"{len(holds)} radios are held off ({sorted(holds)}) — name one "
             f"with --serial")
        return 1
    else:
        keys = list(holds)
    key = keys[0]
    hold = holds[key]
    locations = [(str(h), str(p), bool(e)) for h, p, e in hold.get("locations", [])]
    if not locations:
        _log(f"the hold for {key} records no port locations — clearing it; "
             f"restore the port by hand")
        holds.pop(key, None)
        save_hold(holds)
        return 1

    ok = True
    for hub, port, exact in _ss_first(locations):
        _log(f"switching hub {hub} port {port} ON{' (exact)' if exact else ''}")
        if not _uhubctl_set(hub, port, exact, "on"):
            _log(f"⛔ PORT LEFT OFF on hub {hub} port {port} — every uhubctl "
                 f"form failed; this needs a human at the hub")
            ok = False
    if not ok:
        _log("the hold STAYS so the watchdogs do not fight you while the port "
             "is dark; clear it with --on once the port is back")
        return 1

    # ⛔ WAIT FOR A RUNNING CARD, NOT A PRESENT ONE.  After a real VBUS cut
    # the card's FIRST appearance is its bootloader at 480 Mb/s; starting
    # radiod there gets status=66/NOINPUT.  See wait_for_running_card.
    card, why = wait_for_running_card(locations, hold.get("serial") or None,
                                      timeout_s=wait_seconds)
    if card is None:
        _log(f"⛔ {why}.  The hold STAYS, so the watchdogs will not fight you "
             f"while you look at it; radiod was NOT started.  Run --on again "
             f"once the card is back, or --status to see the hold.")
        return 1
    _log(f"card back: {card.devpath} 04b4:{card.product} at "
         f"{card.speed} Mb/s ({why})")

    radiod_unit = (hold.get("radiod_unit") or unit_for_serial(hold.get("serial"))
                   or cfg.get("unit") or detect_radiod_unit())
    consumers = hold.get("consumers") or []
    restored = True
    if radiod_unit:
        with _with_lifecycle_lock():
            restored = restart_station(radiod_unit, consumers)
    else:
        _log("no radiod instance to start — set [radiod].unit in topology.toml")

    # Clear the hold last: until here a timer tick could have raced the
    # restart and cycled the port under it.
    holds.pop(key, None)
    save_hold(holds)
    _log(f"hold cleared for {key}; automatic recovery is active again")
    return 0 if restored else 1


def manual_status(cfg: dict) -> int:
    holds = load_hold()
    cards = all_rx888()
    if not cards:
        _log("no RX-888 on the bus")
    for card in cards:
        _log(f"card {card.serial or '(no serial)'}: {card.devpath} "
             f"04b4:{card.product} at {card.speed} Mb/s")
    if not holds:
        _log("no manual hold is in force — automatic recovery is active")
        return 0
    for key, hold in sorted(holds.items()):
        since = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ",
                              time.gmtime(hold.get("since", 0)))
        locs = ", ".join(f"hub {h} port {p}" for h, p, _ in hold.get("locations", []))
        _log(f"HELD OFF: {key} since {since} ({locs}); port switching is "
             f"{hold.get('port_power', 'unknown')}.  Restore with "
             f"--on --serial {key}")
    return 0


def main(argv: Optional[Sequence[str]] = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    ap.add_argument("--off-seconds", type=int, default=25,
                    help="how long to hold the port off (default 25)")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                    help="report the decision and change nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--wait-seconds", type=int, default=60,
                    help="with --ensure-present: how long to wait for a slow "
                         "FX3 to finish enumerating before resetting its port "
                         "(default 60)")
    ap.add_argument("--ensure-present", action="store_true",
                    help="installer/bring-up gate: succeed at once if the card "
                         "is on the bus, otherwise recover it NOW (no grace "
                         "window, no cooldown) and wait for it. Exit 0 only if "
                         "the card ends up present.")
    ap.add_argument("--link-blip-seconds", type=int, default=LINK_BLIP_S,
                    help="short power blip used to re-train a card stuck below "
                         "SuperSpeed; deliberately far shorter than "
                         "--off-seconds, which exists to DROP the firmware")
    ap.add_argument("--probe", action="store_true",
                    help="locate the RX-888, check whether its hub port can be "
                         "switched, report, and exit (changes only the state file)")
    ap.add_argument("--off", action="store_true",
                    help="MANUAL: stop radiod and its consumers, switch both "
                         "halves of the card's jack off, confirm the card left "
                         "the bus, and HOLD it off (automatic recovery stands "
                         "down) until --on.  For judging whether a hub really "
                         "cuts VBUS: watch the LED.")
    ap.add_argument("--on", action="store_true",
                    help="MANUAL: undo --off — switch the same ports back on "
                         "(SuperSpeed half first), wait for the card, restart "
                         "radiod and its consumers, and clear the hold")
    ap.add_argument("--status", action="store_true",
                    help="report every RX-888 on the bus and any manual hold")
    ap.add_argument("--on-wait-seconds", type=int, default=MANUAL_ON_WAIT_S,
                    help="with --on: how long to wait for the card to reach "
                         "04b4:00f1 at SuperSpeed before giving up and "
                         f"keeping the hold (default {MANUAL_ON_WAIT_S})")
    ap.add_argument("--serial",
                    help="which RX-888 to act on, by its USB serial; required "
                         "with --off/--on on a station with more than one card")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    cfg = read_config()

    # ── manual power control runs before every automatic guard ───────────
    if sum(bool(x) for x in (args.off, args.on, args.status)) > 1:
        _log("--off, --on and --status are mutually exclusive")
        return 2
    if args.status:
        return manual_status(cfg)
    if args.off:
        return manual_off(args.serial, cfg)
    if args.on:
        return manual_on(args.serial, cfg, wait_seconds=args.on_wait_seconds)

    patterns = cfg.get("consumers", [])
    radiod_unit = cfg.get("unit") or detect_radiod_unit()

    # ⛔ A MANUAL HOLD OUTRANKS EVERY AUTOMATIC PATH.  An operator who ran
    # --off took the port down deliberately — to watch the LED, swap a cable,
    # or move the card.  A timer tick that "recovers" it turns the port back
    # on under their hands and, worse, makes the hub look like it recovered
    # the card by itself.  Nothing below this point runs while a hold stands.
    held = held_serials()
    if held:
        _log(f"manual hold in force for {', '.join(held)} ({HOLD_PATH}) — "
             f"standing down.  Release with: sigmond-sdr-recover --on")
        return 0

    present = device_present()
    state = load_state()
    now = time.time()

    # Answer the gate's question before any other guard can return early for
    # an unrelated reason: --ensure-present asks only "is the card on the
    # bus", and on a station that has no radiod instance yet (the exact case
    # this gate exists for) the radiod guard below would otherwise pre-empt it.
    if args.ensure_present:
        if present:
            _log("card is on the bus — nothing to do")
            return 0
        # Absent is not the same as latched.  An RX-888 enumerates first as
        # its FX3 bootloader (04b4:00f3) and only becomes 04b4:00f1 once the
        # firmware loader promotes it -- and on a fresh install bring-up
        # installs that firmware and reloads udev in the same run, so the card
        # can be seconds away from appearing.  Resetting the port at that moment
        # throws away a card that was about to arrive (AI6VN, v3.42,
        # 2026-09-17: config init found no SDR, the card was healthy on the
        # bus a minute later).  So wait first, and only cycle if it really
        # does not come.
        for _ in range(int(args.wait_seconds / 5) or 1):
            time.sleep(5)
            if device_present():
                _log(f"card appeared while waiting — nothing to recover")
                return 0
        _log(f"card still absent after {args.wait_seconds}s — recovering")

    # ── learn the hub port while the card is present ──────────────────────
    if cfg.get("recover_hub") and cfg.get("recover_port"):
        hub, port = str(cfg["recover_hub"]), str(cfg["recover_port"])
        source = f"{CONFIG_PATH} [radiod]"
    else:
        if present:
            loc = locate_rx888()
            # ⚡ A CARD ON THE WRONG BUS IS NOT A DEAD CARD.  If it came back
            # from its bootloader without re-training to SuperSpeed, radiod
            # refuses it and the station reads as a hardware failure while the
            # card sits there healthy.  One short blip fixes it; see
            # promote_link().  Do this BEFORE anything is learned from loc,
            # so the location recorded is the one the card ends up on -- it
            # moves bus when the promotion works (2-1.3 -> 4-1.3).
            if link_underspeed(loc) and not args.dry_run:
                promoted = promote_link(loc, args.link_blip_seconds)
                if promoted is not None:
                    loc = promoted
                    # ⚠ NOT an sdr_events record.  Its KINDS are LOSS_KINDS
                    # plus the arrival, and episode logic keys off that: a
                    # link promotion is neither a loss nor an arrival but a
                    # repair, and inventing a kind here would change how
                    # episodes open and close.  promote_link() already logs
                    # the before/after, which is the evidence that matters.
                    # Making it first-class belongs with a decision about
                    # episode semantics, not with this fix.
                if link_underspeed(loc):
                    log_on_change(state,
                        f"the card is linked at {loc.speed} Mb/s, not {SS_SPEED}, "
                        f"and a blip did not promote it — this hub does not follow "
                        f"the bootloader-to-SuperSpeed transition. radiod will "
                        f"refuse the card. Try another hub port, another cable, "
                        f"or a hub known to follow it.")
            stale = (state.get("devpath") != (loc.devpath if loc else None)
                     or state.get("switchable") is None
                     or now - float(state.get("verified_at") or 0) > REVERIFY_S)
            if loc and stale:
                sw, ctl = hub_switchable(loc)
                halves = port_halves(loc, ctl) if sw else {}
                power, _ = port_power(halves, cfg)
                state.update(devpath=loc.devpath, serial=loc.serial,
                             speed=loc.speed, switchable=sw, verified_at=now,
                             hub=loc.hub, port=loc.port,
                             # every location uhubctl accepted for this port
                             locations=[list(c) for c in ctl],
                             halves=halves, power=power)
                claim = {"usb2-only": "uhubctl reaches only the USB2 half of "
                                      "this port — a port reset cannot clear "
                                      "the RX888, so a vanish, a latch or a "
                                      "wedge asks for a hand replug at once; "
                                      "move the card to a hub whose SuperSpeed "
                                      "half uhubctl can switch",
                         "data-only": "VBUS stays up through a cut here — a "
                                      "port reset can clear a stream wedge, "
                                      "while a vanish or a latch asks for a "
                                      "hand replug at once"}
                verdict = {True: claim.get(power,
                                           f"{port_claim(cfg)} — a port reset is possible"),
                           False: "uhubctl cannot switch this port — a port "
                                  "reset is impossible from software; move the "
                                  "card to a uhubctl-capable hub port",
                           None: "port switching UNKNOWN (uhubctl could not "
                                 "look — run as root, is uhubctl installed?)"}
                via = (" (control: " + ", ".join(
                       f"{h} p{p}{' exact' if e else ''}" for h, p, e in ctl) + ")"
                       if ctl else "")
                _log(f"RX-888 {loc.serial} at {loc.devpath} ({loc.speed} Mb/s) "
                     f"on hub {loc.hub} port {loc.port}: {verdict[sw]}{via}")
        hub, port = state.get("hub"), state.get("port")
        source = "discovered" if hub else "nothing learned yet"
    locations = [tuple(l) for l in state.get("locations") or []] \
        if not cfg.get("recover_hub") else [(hub, port, False)]
    bootstrapped = False

    if args.probe:
        _log(f"hub={hub} port={port} ({source}) switchable={state.get('switchable')} "
             f"power={_power_now(state, cfg)[0]} "
             f"control={locations or 'none'} serial={state.get('serial')} "
             f"radiod_unit={radiod_unit} consumers={patterns or 'NONE DECLARED'}")
        save_state(state)
        return 0

    if not (hub and port) and not present:
        # Bootstrap: never seen, nothing learned -- but a latched card is
        # visible as a device-less port on a switchable hub.  See
        # bootstrap_locations().
        boot = bootstrap_locations()
        if boot:
            locations = boot
            hub, port = boot[0][0], boot[0][1]
            state["switchable"] = True
            bootstrapped = True
            _log("bootstrap: the card was never seen here, so no port was "
                 "learned; resetting every device-less port on a switchable "
                 "hub (a latched RX-888 looks exactly like that, and a port "
                 "with a working device on it is never a candidate): "
                 + ", ".join(f"{h} p{p}" for h, p, _ in boot))
    if not (hub and port):
        msg = ("no hub/port to cycle: nothing in [radiod] and the "
               "card was never seen on a bus this helper could inspect — "
               + ("it is absent right now; cannot recover" if not present
                  else "unexpected while present"))
        if args.dry_run:        # a person asked: answer, and leave "said" alone
            _log(msg)
        else:
            log_on_change(state, msg)
        save_state(state)
        return 0
    if not radiod_unit and not bootstrapped:
        save_state(state)
        _log("no radiod unit: [radiod].unit unset and not exactly one active "
             "radiod@ instance — set it in topology.toml")
        return 0
    if not radiod_unit and bootstrapped:
        # Cycling is what makes the card exist; radiod is configured after.
        _log("no radiod unit yet — resetting the port anyway to bring the card back, "
             "then bring-up can configure radiod against real hardware")
    if state.get("switchable") is False and not cfg.get("recover_hub"):
        if not present:
            msg = (f"card absent but hub {hub} port {port} cannot "
                   f"be switched — a manual replug is the only recovery")
            if args.dry_run:    # a person asked: answer, and leave "said" alone
                _log(msg)
            else:
                log_on_change(state, msg)
        elif not args.dry_run:
            state.pop("said", None)
        save_state(state)
        return 0

    if present:
        if state.get("absent_since"):
            _log("device returned to the bus")
            state["card_return_at"] = now
            state.pop("said", None)     # re-arm "holding" for the next absence
        state["absent_since"] = None
    elif not state.get("absent_since"):
        state["absent_since"] = now
        _log("device absent from the bus — starting the grace window")

    absent_for = (now - state["absent_since"]) if state.get("absent_since") else 0.0
    last_cycle = state.get("last_cycle_at")
    if args.ensure_present:
        # A gate, not a watchdog.  The grace window exists so a re-enumeration
        # blip is not mistaken for a failure, and the cooldown so a hardware
        # fault cannot become a port-reset loop; neither applies when a
        # human or the installer is asking, once, "make the card present".
        # Without this a FRESH INSTALL sits with a latched card and no radiod:
        # bring-up configures radiod against hardware that is not there, the
        # station comes up dead, and the timer's own grace/cooldown mean it is
        # minutes before anything even tries (AI6VN, v3.40, 2026-09-17).
        decision = Decision(True, "ensure-present: card absent — recovering now")
    else:
        decision = should_cycle(present, absent_for,
                                (now - last_cycle) if last_cycle else None)

    # v3.72 #3: a card that is present but wedged, and the latch brake.
    card_now = locate_rx888() if present else None
    if card_now is not None and card_now.devnum:
        if state.get("devnum") and state["devnum"] != card_now.devnum:
            state["card_return_at"] = now       # it re-enumerated: a replug or a reset
        state["devnum"] = card_now.devnum
    if not present:
        state.pop("usb2_key", None)
    if not present and radiod_unit:
        sample_nrestarts(radiod_unit, state, now)   # keep the delta fresh
    wedged = (not args.ensure_present and present
              and radiod_is_wedged(radiod_unit, state, now, card=card_now))
    if not args.ensure_present:
        if state.get("latched"):
            healthy = classify_return(card_now, [])[0] == "recovered" if card_now else False
            if present and healthy and not wedged:
                _log("the card is back on a SuperSpeed path — resets allowed again")
                state.pop("latched", None)
                state.pop("replug_alarm_at", None)
            else:
                decision = Decision(False, "the RX888 needs a hand replug — "
                                    "not resetting until it is back healthy")
        elif present and wedged and not decision.cycle:
            if last_cycle and now - last_cycle < WEDGE_RESET_COOLDOWN_S:
                decision = Decision(False, f"card on the bus but radiod is wedged; "
                                    f"last reset {now - last_cycle:.0f}s ago, holding "
                                    f"off until {WEDGE_RESET_COOLDOWN_S}s")
            else:
                decision = Decision(True, f"card on the bus but radiod is wedged "
                                    f"({wedged}) — resetting its hub port")

    # v3.73 row 9: on a port where no reset can clear this fault, ask for the
    # hand replug at once instead of resetting and waiting.  AC0G-B4, 10-15: a
    # reset that reached only the hub's USB2 half left the RX888 latched.
    if decision.cycle and not bootstrapped:
        power, pwhy = _power_now(state, cfg)
        if not present:
            trigger = "absent"
        elif card_now is not None and classify_return(card_now, [])[0] == "recovered":
            trigger = "wedge"
        else:
            trigger = "latched"
        if not reset_can_help(power, trigger):
            why = f"{pwhy}, so a port reset cannot clear {_TRIGGER_WORDS[trigger]}"
            if args.dry_run:
                _log(f"holding: the RX888 needs a hand replug — {why} "
                     f"(dry run: no alarm recorded)")
                return 0
            _raise_replug_alarm(state, now, why, tried=False)
            return 1 if args.ensure_present else 0

    if not decision.cycle:
        # Report when the card is missing, and always under --dry-run: someone
        # who asks what this would do deserves an answer even when the answer
        # is "nothing".  Silence reads as a broken tool.  The timer's own runs
        # say it once per change, never once per 30 s tick.
        if args.dry_run:
            _log(f"holding: {decision.reason}")
        elif not present or state.get("latched") or wedged:
            log_on_change(state, f"holding: {decision.reason}")
        save_state(state)
        return 0

    _log(decision.reason)
    if args.dry_run:
        _log("dry run — no port reset performed")
        return 0

    # ⛔ LEARNED LOCATIONS ARE BUS PATHS, AND BUS PATHS MOVE ACROSS A REBOOT.
    # "5-1" is not a hub, it is wherever the kernel happened to enumerate a hub
    # this boot.  AI6VN, 2026-09-29: the card was learned at hub 4-1 port 4
    # while 4-1 WAS the USB3 hub; after a reboot the buses renumbered so that
    # 4-1 became the USB2 hub and the card's real home became 5-1.  The card
    # had already vanished by the time the system came up, so there was never a
    # moment with the card present in which to re-learn -- and the helper then
    # dutifully reset the USB2 hub's port 4, which was EMPTY, once per
    # attempt, while the card sat on the SuperSpeed half.  rob
    # replugged the cable by hand and it came straight back at 5 Gbps.
    #
    # So when the card is ABSENT, never trust the learned path alone.  Union it
    # with every device-less port on every switchable hub -- which is what
    # bootstrap_locations() already finds, and which by construction includes
    # the card's real port however the buses were numbered this boot.  A port
    # with a working device on it is never in that set, so the GPSDO, the TS-1
    # and the magnetometer are never touched.
    targets = list(locations or [])
    if not present:
        extra = [l for l in bootstrap_locations() if l not in targets]
        if extra:
            _log(f"card absent — also cycling every device-less port, since a "
                 f"learned bus path can point at different hardware after a "
                 f"reboot: {extra}")
            targets += extra
    if not targets:
        targets = [(hub, str(port), False)]
    # ⛔ Never reset a port that holds anything but the RX-888: the reset
    # drops the data link of everything on it, and a GPSDO loses lock.
    # Only the occupied ports drop out; the refusal is not an attempt, so it
    # sets no cooldown, and it is logged once per change.
    targets, dirty = partition_targets(targets)
    if dirty:
        msg = ("REFUSING the port reset on "
               + ", ".join(f"hub {h} port {p}: " + ", ".join(f"{d} ({i})" for d, i in o)
                           for (h, p, _), o in dirty)
               + " — it shares the port (either half) with the RX-888 and "
                 "would be dropped too")
        log_on_change(state, msg)
    if not targets:
        save_state(state)
        return 1 if args.ensure_present else 0
    # ⛔ DO NOT SPEND A CYCLE -- OR A COOLDOWN -- ON A PORT THAT IS NOT THERE.
    # See live_targets(): a learned path that no longer resolves is the exact
    # shape of AI6VN's 2026-10-21 failure, where the cooldown that followed
    # hid a working hub behind a stale address.
    # ⚠ REPORT staleness; do NOT withhold the attempt on it.  "uhubctl did
    # not confirm this port" is not "this port does not exist" -- it is also
    # what a stubbed, unreadable or merely unlucky probe looks like, and a
    # probe that cannot confirm must never be the reason a vanished card goes
    # unrecovered.  So the cycle still runs on everything; what changes is
    # that a stale address is NAMED, and an all-stale pass does not burn a
    # cooldown it would spend hiding the real problem.
    live, stale = live_targets(targets)
    if stale:
        log_on_change(state, "learned port(s) uhubctl can no longer address: "
                      + ", ".join(f"hub {h} port {p}" for h, p, _ in stale)
                      + " — the bus has renumbered since they were learned; "
                        "cycling them anyway, but this is the AI6VN 2026-10-21 "
                        "shape, where a stale path hid a working hub")
    state["last_cycle_at"] = now
    state.pop("said", None)     # after a reset, the next "holding" carries news
    save_state(state)
    # Record before the reset: a failed one still counts as an attempt, and
    # the record survives a helper that dies mid-reset.
    note_event("port-reset", " ".join(f"{h}p{p}" for h, p, _ in targets))
    if not cycle_port(targets, args.off_seconds):
        if not live:
            # Nothing we switched was even addressable, so this was not a real
            # attempt: clear the cooldown stamp rather than hold off, and force
            # a fresh probe so the next pass can find the card's true address.
            state.pop("last_cycle_at", None)
            state.pop("switchable", None)
            log_on_change(state, "NO ADDRESSABLE PORT was cycled — every learned "
                          "location is stale. Not holding off: a cooldown here "
                          "would hide the fault. If this repeats, the card or "
                          "its hub needs replugging at the machine.")
            save_state(state)
        return 1
    t_on = time.time()      # the port is back ON: only the kernel's words after this count
    state["card_return_at"] = t_on
    if not wait_for_device():
        _raise_replug_alarm(state, now, "the card never returned to the bus "
                            "after the port reset")
        if args.ensure_present:
            _log("card did not return after the port reset — check the cable "
                 "and the hub's own power; bring-up cannot configure radiod "
                 "against hardware that is not on the bus")
            return 1
        _log("card did not return after the port reset — hardware fault, "
             "escalating by leaving the station down rather than looping")
        return 1

    # Classify the return.  00f3 first, then 00f1: give the firmware a moment
    # to start before judging, and never read the bootloader id as success.
    card = None
    for _ in range(10):
        card = locate_rx888()
        if card is not None and card.product == "00f1":
            break
        time.sleep(2)
    verdict, why = classify_return(card, kernel_latch_errors(t_on, port_prefixes(targets)))
    if verdict != "recovered":
        _raise_replug_alarm(state, now, why)
        return 1

    units = installed_units()
    if not patterns:
        _log("NO CONSUMERS DECLARED in [radiod].consumers — only radiod will be "
             "restarted; recorders keep a stale RTP anchor until restarted by "
             "hand (or by a PartOf= drop-in).  Declare them in topology.toml.")
    consumers, missing = expand_consumers_checked(patterns, units)
    for pattern in missing:
        _log(f"DECLARED CONSUMER NOT FOUND: {pattern} matched no installed "
             f"unit — it will not be restarted; check topology.toml")
    if not radiod_unit:
        _log("card recovery done; no radiod unit to start yet — run "
             "`smd bringup` / the wizard now that the card is present")
        save_state(state)
        return 0
    with _with_lifecycle_lock():
        ok = restart_station(radiod_unit, consumers)
    if ok:
        _log(f"recovered by port reset ({why}; radiod ready)")
        state.pop("latched", None)
        state.pop("replug_alarm_at", None)
        save_state(state)
    _log("station restored" if ok else "station NOT fully restored")
    return 0 if ok else 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
